The storage service's integration tests have become flaky on the CI runners, and I have narrowed the flakiness to a single test file that exercises the compaction scheduler. The team agreed that the fastest way to get useful evidence is to run just that test repeatedly and capture which of the runs fail, how long the failing runs take, and whether the failures cluster at a particular test case. The test file lives under the storage service's module: the repository root is /home/runner/storage, the test module is com.internal.storage.CompactionSchedulerTest, and the build uses Gradle. The exact Gradle task is not important as long as the invocation selects that test class and does not run the rest of the suite; the scheduler tests share a fake clock that other tests manipulate, so running the whole module would reintroduce the interference we are trying to isolate. I want the run bounded: a CI runner is allowed a fixed budget, and a test that hangs should fail rather than occupy the worker forever. Something in the region of five minutes for a single pass is enough, since a healthy pass of that class takes under a minute. The command should run the test once, print the standard test report to the console, and exit with the build's status. I am deliberately not running it in a loop from the shell; one controlled pass first, then we decide whether to repeat it. I do not need the test modified, skipped, or re-run with retries yet. The repository is already checked out and the Gradle wrapper is present, so no dependency download step is needed beyond what Gradle resolves from its cache. The next step is to execute that single test class through the build tool. Return the single tool call that performs the next step
